TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"partial","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R11","met":"no","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou latitude given as 45.05°N; actual is about 5.2°N, and the equatorial-launch argument is then inconsistent."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Ω = 27.33° is said to indicate the launch facility location, which is not justified for a sun-synchronous RAAN, and the 1°/day precession claim is misstated (sun-synchronous precession is about 0.9856°/day, not 360°/year as a requirement phrased that way)."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Retrograde orbit claim 'passes over almost the entire Earth' and 'frequent orbital period' are vague; no period or revs/day given."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag sail alternative sentence is truncated; uncontrolled deorbit at 600 km would not burn up fully and is not evaluated quantitatively."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant described as having two radio telescopes with receivers; claim unsupported and ground station selection reasoning contradicts 'close to poles' rule."}],"overall":"Coherent concept with STK access and ground-track evidence, but significant technical errors (launch site latitude, RAAN reasoning), incomplete users, orbit element and EOL analysis, truncated text, and prohibited Wikipedia citation. Adequate overall.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Mission purpose, rationale and single-spacecraft choice are given with some background. Users (operator and customers) are only implied via 'operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities'; no explicit operator/customer identification. Referencing is largely web links, some Wikipedia-sourced, weakening support.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Hundreds of millions of humans rely on reefs for food, natural protection, jobs, and tourism."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Covers launch, orbit, COE table, ground station and STK access, EOL. However, the launch site latitude (45.05°N) is wrong for Kourou (about 5°N), the 'equator' rationale is inconsistent, Vega E is unbuilt, RAAN of 27.33° is unjustified, and the 'Argument of Latitude' is set to 0 with no epoch. Access listing has duplicated numbering (13). Mount Pleasant is not a radio observatory with two telescopes as stated; EOL section lacks a clear decision and has a truncated sentence.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of 87.7924° for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":4.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions summarise the design and note launch risk, but are generic and do not draw quantitative conclusions from the STK results or EOL analysis.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear headings and labelled STK figures, but Wikipedia is cited despite prohibition, reference style is inconsistent, there are typos and a broken sentence in EOL, and the executive summary is not concise, with a truncated drag-sail sentence.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The proposed ZYZZsat is small and without thrusters in a low-earth orbit."}]}]}
